 % ABSTRACT (English)
-This project aims to design and develop a Voice-Controlled Ship Navigation Game as a learning tool to enhance communication skills and reduce foreign language anxiety among learners. The system integrates a simulation game format with Speech Recognition technology and consists of five main systems: the voice command reception and processing system, the ship physics and control system, the AI crew system, the random obstacle generation system, and the user interface. It supports the user in the role of a ship captain (the player) who must communicate with and issue commands to an AI crew, specifically the navigator and the helmsman. The game is developed using the Unity 3D engine and C\#, utilizing natural language processing alongside a speech recognition model such as Whisper AI to convert English voice commands into text for processing. The AI crew system enables the simulation of communication breakdowns; if the player's pronunciation is unclear or a command is out of context, the crew will directly request clarification or repetition. This effectively enhances speaking fluency and real-time problem-solving skills under pressure. The system's performance and user experience will be evaluated using assessment forms from a test group of 40-50 participants.
+This project aims to design and develop a voice-controlled ship navigation game as a learning tool to enhance communication skills and reduce foreign language anxiety among learners. The system integrates a simulation game format with speech recognition technology and consists of the following main systems: the speech recognition and command processing system, the ship physics and control system, the AI crew system, the obstacle generation system, the user interface, the language support system, the story mode, and the research logging system. The player takes the role of a ship captain who must communicate with and issue standard marine orders to an AI crew, namely the chief engineer, who runs the engine, and the helmsman and lookout, who steers the ship and watches for hazards. The game is developed with the Unity engine and C\#. English voice commands are transcribed on the player's computer by the Whisper model via whisper.cpp; unclear commands are interpreted with GPT-4o mini. The AI crew system simulates communication breakdowns: if the player's pronunciation is unclear or a command is out of context, the crew asks the player to confirm or repeat the order. The game is therefore designed to give players practice in speaking fluently and solving problems under time pressure. At this progress stage, a working prototype has been built, all 363 of 363 unit tests pass, and the developer's own system tests have been completed; the user evaluation is planned for the next phase. The system's performance will be measured from in-game logs, and the speaking anxiety of 40--50 participants will be compared before and after play using a 12-item scale adapted from the Foreign Language Classroom Anxiety Scale (FLCAS), together with a game experience questionnaire.
